<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Life Adventures – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Small, Brown Bag.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Relay simple information received verbally using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information in texts to convey meaning by pointing to images that match the story (e.g., points to a picture of a lost book to indicate, "The story is about a book."). Can relay simple information received verbally by repeating short words or using gestures (e.g., hears "Sit down." and points to a chair). Can point to classroom objects to help peers understand the vocabulary (e.g., points to "pencil" when asked).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
                Engagement, Modeling and Clarification
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up various classroom objects (realia: a small brown bag, a red book, a blue pencil) and asks, "What is this?" Students respond with one-word answers or point. The teacher then says, "Point to the small brown bag!" and students point. This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and connects to the theme visually and physically.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to understand a description and point to the correct object. For example, the teacher says, "Look! It's a big, red book!" while pointing clearly to a big red book. The teacher then points to a 'small brown bag' and says, "This is a small, brown bag." and encourages students to repeat. The teacher also models using gestures to relay meaning by, for instance, silently picking up a pencil and pointing to paper, indicating "write." Phonemic awareness: The teacher exaggerates the /b/ sound in "book" and "bag" and the /s/ sound in "small," having students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a blue book) "Is this a red book?" (Expected answer: "No," or shake head)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a small pencil) "Is it big or small?" (Expected answer: "Small")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures"
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a "Lost and Found" poster (visual text) with simple drawings of lost classroom objects (e.g., a "small blue pencil," a "big yellow bag," a "round red eraser"). Each image has a very short, simple description like "Lost! A small blue pencil." or "Find! My big yellow bag." (Genre: simple poster/notice, Register: informal, Format: visual with short sentences).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will use visual information in texts to convey meaning and relay simple information received verbally by pointing or using short words. They will understand and respond to simple WH-questions related to the objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the poster about?" (Expected: "Lost things," "School objects," or pointing to objects). "See the pencil?" (Expected: "Yes").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Mediation):</b> The teacher says, "Point to the small blue pencil on the poster." Students point. "Point to the big yellow bag." Students point. The teacher asks, "What color is the bag?" (Expected: "Yellow"). The teacher then asks, "What size is the pencil?" (Expected: "Small").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                Accuracy focus on Mediation
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating information by matching descriptions to visuals, repeating instructions, and using simple adjectives (brown, red, blue, yellow, green, black, white, big, small) and nouns (book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, eraser, ruler) with present simple ("That's a...") and demonstratives ("This," "That"). Phonemic awareness: focus on initial /p/ in "pencil."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice: "Listen and Circle" (Visual Mediation):</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing rows of 3-4 different classroom object images (e.g., a small red book, a big blue book, a small green book). The teacher gives a clear description, for example, "Circle the small red book." Students listen and circle the correct image. Repeat with 5-6 different objects, ensuring variety in color and size (e.g., "Find the long yellow ruler," "Circle the big black board").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice: "Describe and Find" (Verbal to Visual Mediation):</b> The teacher displays flashcards of classroom objects around the room. The teacher describes an object using two adjectives and a noun (e.g., "It's a small, brown bag"). Students listen, find the object's flashcard, and point to it. Some students can be encouraged to say the object's name. The teacher prompts, "What is it?" (Expected: "A small brown bag."). Incorporate polite expressions: "Can you please point to the small brown bag?" "Thank you!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction: "My Partner Says" (Peer Mediation):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of 5-6 classroom object flashcards. Student A picks a flashcard and describes it using simple English, (e.g., "It's big and blue. What is it?"). Student B listens, points to the correct flashcard, and says the object's name (e.g., "It's a big blue backpack!"). They can switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging clear descriptions and correct pointing, prompting for "Is it this?" or "Is it that?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                Communicative task using language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures"
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: Classroom Object Bingo Game (Project 2: Classroom Object Bingo).</b> Students will play a game of Bingo, directly applying their mediation skills to understand and respond to verbal descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes pre-made Bingo cards to each student. Each card has a grid of 9 or 12 different classroom object images (e.g., a "small brown bag," a "red book," a "blue pencil," a "green eraser," a "yellow ruler," a "white board," a "black marker," a "pink crayon," "three chairs").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the rules in simple English, demonstrating with one card: "I will describe an object. Listen carefully! If you have the picture, mark it with your crayon or a small paper square. When you have three in a row (or four, depending on card size), say 'Bingo!'" The teacher uses gestures for "three in a row" and "mark."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher begins to call out descriptions of classroom objects, varying adjectives, colors, and nouns, ensuring all target vocabulary and grammar points are used (e.g., "Find the small, brown bag.", "Look for the big, red book.", "Point to the long, yellow ruler.", "That's a blue pencil."). The teacher may use polite expressions like "Can you find the blue pencil, please?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the verbal descriptions (input) and mediate this information to identify and mark the corresponding visual on their Bingo card (output). If a student struggles, the teacher can repeat the description or point to a similar real object as a clue (e.g., "It's like this one, but blue.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first student(s) to get a "Bingo!" wins. The teacher verifies their card matches the called descriptions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                Formative assessment strategy
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Bingo Card Check.</b> The teacher observes students during the Bingo game and collects their Bingo cards for a quick check. An observation checklist focuses on active listening and accurate visual mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to correctly mark the described objects on their Bingo cards. When a student calls "Bingo!", the teacher asks them to point to and (if able) name the marked objects to confirm understanding and production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation (Visual):</b> Student correctly points to or marks the described object on their Bingo card (e.g., marks "small brown bag" when described).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary (Verbal/Pointing):</b> Student can point to specific classroom objects when asked (e.g., points to "pencil" when prompted) or repeat simple words (e.g., "red book").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation & Collaboration:</b> Student actively listens and participates in the game, attempting to follow instructions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a thumbs up/down, or circle happy/sad face) "Did you understand the names of the objects?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you find the pictures on your Bingo card?" (Yes/No or point)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite object in our Bingo game today?" (Students can point to their card or a real object).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you learn today?" (If applicable, or "What word did you remember well?")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of students mediating information from verbal descriptions to visual identification? The Bingo game was highly engaging and effective for practicing mediation. Most students successfully understood simple two-adjective descriptions and matched them to images. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson or future lessons? Some students still relied on peer observation to find objects; future activities could involve more individual descriptive prompts. Ensure the descriptions are consistently clear and the pace is appropriate for 2nd graders. Reinforce polite expressions during the game ("Thank you for playing!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "School Life Adventures" theme by consolidating vocabulary and practicing mediation skills through a fun game. Future lessons in a new theme will build upon these foundational skills, requiring students to continue using visual cues and simple language to understand and convey information in new contexts, potentially mediating between texts and actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>